  "query_preview": "..\nIssued 1100 PM CDT Wed Sep 9 2026\n\nFriday night through Wednesday:\n\nFriday night will feature high pressure exiting the region with\na low pressure system pushing in from the west sliding \nnortheast with nearly zonal flow aloft. As the low lifts north \nof the Great Lakes a trailing front will pull through the region\nlate Friday night through Saturday. The concern with this is \nthe overall forcing becomes a little weaker aloft and limited \nmoisture in the low to mid levels. It improves later Saturday\ninto Saturday evening so there will be increased potential but\noverall some uncertainty in exactly whether will see precip from\nthis. Some storms would be possible with this but overall no\nstrong storms would be expected given limited shear. \n\nA brief period of high pressure should keep things quiet through\nthe day Sunday but a developing low in the central US should\nstart to bring some precip potential in overnight with a decent\nshortwave pushing through though the focus will be further\nnorthwest at this time associated with strong WAA from a strong\nLLJ. Into the day Monday expect the low to lift into northern WI\nwith the trailing front becoming the concern later in the day.\nWhile its no a guarantee given relatively uncertain shear this\nfront will carry some risk for strong to severe storms with\ndecent instability and depending on where storms are relative to\nthe front could be enough shear for storm organization and thus\nsevere weather. This will be something to watch going into the\nweekend.\n\nBeyond Monday looks likely to be drier through Wednesday as\nhigher pressure pushes in behind the front. Things become more\nuncertain after Wednesday.\n\nKuroski",
